    "lead": "For years, investors said Indians won't pay for content. Kuku FM, an app for audio stories and 1–2 minute phone dramas, just showed a ₹183 crore profit on ₹1,484 crore revenue, 6× more than a year ago. Over 99% of it came from subscriptions.",
    "body": "The old belief was simple: Indians want everything free, so apps can only earn from ads. Three things changed at once. First, a ban created a gap. In August 2025 India banned real-money gaming, and Lumikai counted India's gamer base falling from 609 million to 555 million. Those users, and the habit of spending small amounts on a phone, had to go somewhere. A lot of it went to microdramas: short, cliffhanger shows watched on a vertical screen. That market is now worth about $300M with 100 million monthly users, up from almost nothing a year earlier. Second, paying got easy. UPI Autopay lets someone set up a ₹99 or ₹199 plan in two taps, with no card. That's why Kuku's ₹1,475 crore of subscription money came almost entirely from India. Third, AI made stories cheap to make. Pocket FM, Kuku's rival, says AI now helps produce 93% of its catalogue, and its yearly revenue run-rate doubled to about $430M by April. Here's the twist. Kuku is still a heavy spender: it put ₹1,105 crore, about three-quarters of its costs, into ads. It is profitable anyway, because each paying listener now brings back more than they cost to win. Cheap AI shows plus small UPI payments turned 'Indians won't pay' into 'Indians pay ₹99 at a time, every month'.",
